  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThis Afternoon/Tonight...\n\nScattered showers have largely come to an end across the area, \nthough isolated activity can't be ruled out across far southeastern \nportions of the area. Further northwest lingering stratus is favored \nto gradually clear throughout the afternoon-early evening hours. \nScattered thunderstorm redevelopment along/north of a cold front in \nKansas could clip southern portions of Rooks/Osborne/Mitchell \ncounties during the late evening-early overnight hours. Otherwise \nmostly clear skies are expected overnight with lows in the mid 60s. \n\nThursday...\n\nLargely zonal flow aloft continues across the central U.S. on \nThursday. Highs will be near their climatological normals, in the \nlow 80s. Breezy southerly winds gusting 20-30mph are expected during \nthe afternoon hours across northwest portions of the area. Overall a \nfairly pleasant September day is in store for the area. \n\nFriday...\n\nTemperatures climb into the upper 80s-low 90s on Friday ahead of an \napproaching shortwave trough. Southerly winds will be breezy during \nthe day, gusting 25-30mph, strongest for areas northeast of the Tri-\nCities. The breezy southerly flow will also help to advect moisture \ninto the area ahead of an approaching cold front with dewpoints \nclimbing into the upper 60s-low 70s. Isolated-scattered thunderstorm \ndevelopment is possible during the late evening-overnight hours\nalong the cold front. There is some uncertainty how far south \nstorms will be able to develop given weaker forcing (front \nfurther from surface low) and stronger capping. Still, at least \nisolated development is possible across the area. If storms do \nform, 2000+ J/Kg of CAPE and 30kts of shear would support \nstrong/severe thunderstorms. The cold front will gradually move \ninto the area during the overnight hours with winds becoming \nnortherly behind the front. \n\nThis Weekend Onwards...\n\nThe aforementioned cold front looks to stall out across southern \nportions of the area during the day on Saturday. North of the front, \ncooler and breezy conditions are expected with highs in the upper \n80s south of the front. Scattered thunderstorms are possible \nalong/north of the front during the afternoon/evening hours on \nSaturday, some of which may be strong-severe though impacts remain \nuncertain given spread in model guidance on the placement of the \nfront. Off and on thunderstorm chances continue through the end of \nthe forecast period as a series of disturbances move thorugh the \narea. Highs will vary, ranging from the low 70s to the low 90s \ndepending on the timing of frontal passages.",
